::ALG1_U03_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which boundary line is used to graph y > 3x - 2? {
=A. y = 3x - 2#Correct. Replace the inequality symbol with an equals sign to find the boundary.
~B. y = -3x + 2#This changes both the slope and intercept signs.
~C. y = 3x + 2#The intercept sign is wrong.
~D. x = 3y - 2#This switches the variables and is not the boundary line.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What line type should be used for y <= 4x + 1? {
~A. Dashed, because the symbol points left#Line type is based on whether equality is included, not symbol direction.
~B. Dashed, because y is isolated#Having y isolated does not determine line type.
=C. Solid, because equality is included#Correct. The symbol <= includes the boundary line.
~D. Solid, because the slope is positive#Slope direction does not determine line type.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality uses a dashed boundary line? {
~A. y <= x + 2#The symbol <= includes equality, so the line is solid.
=B. y < 5x - 1#Correct. The symbol < does not include equality, so the line is dashed.
~C. y >= -x#The symbol >= includes equality, so the line is solid.
~D. y = 2x + 3#This is an equation, not an inequality.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which description matches y >= 2x - 5? {
~A. Dashed line y = 2x - 5, shade below#The boundary should be solid and shading should be above.
~B. Solid line y = 2x - 5, shade below#The boundary is included, but >= means shade above.
~C. Dashed line y = 2x - 5, shade above#The shading is right, but equality requires a solid line.
=D. Solid line y = 2x - 5, shade above#Correct. The graph includes the boundary and the region above it.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Does (0, 0) satisfy y < x + 1? {
=A. Yes, because 0 < 1 is true#Correct. Substitute x = 0 and y = 0 to get 0 < 1.
~B. Yes, because 0 = 1 is true#0 = 1 is false, and the inequality is not an equation.
~C. No, because 0 is not less than 0#The right side is x + 1, which equals 1.
~D. No, because the boundary is dashed#A dashed boundary does not mean all points are excluded.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y >= -x + 2? {
~A. (0, 1)#Substitution gives 1 >= 2, which is false.
=B. (0, 2)#Correct. Substitution gives 2 >= 2, and equality is allowed.
~C. (1, 0)#Substitution gives 0 >= 1, which is false.
~D. (2, -1)#Substitution gives -1 >= 0, which is false.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>For y < -2x + 4, where should the graph be shaded? {
~A. Above the boundary line#Above represents y greater than the expression.
~B. On the boundary line only#The solution region includes a side of the line, not only the boundary.
~C. To the right of the y-axis#Shading is relative to the boundary line, not the y-axis.
=D. Below the boundary line#Correct. The symbol < means y-values less than the boundary expression.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph description matches y > 1/2x + 3? {
~A. Solid boundary, shade above#The shading is right, but > uses a dashed boundary.
~B. Dashed boundary, shade below#The line type is right, but > means shade above.
~C. Solid boundary, shade below#Both the line type and shading are wrong.
=D. Dashed boundary, shade above#Correct. Greater than uses a dashed boundary and shades above.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>A region represents 2x + 5y <= 30. What does the boundary represent? {
=A. Combinations that cost exactly 30#Correct. Replacing <= with = gives the exact-limit boundary.
~B. Combinations that cost more than 30#Those points are outside the at-most solution region.
~C. Only combinations where x = 0#The boundary includes many ordered pairs, not only x = 0.
~D. Only combinations where y = 0#The boundary includes many ordered pairs, not only y = 0.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>A club needs at least 40 points. Small tasks are worth 4 points and large tasks are worth 10 points. Which inequality models the situation? {
~A. 4x + 10y <= 40#At least means the total should be greater than or equal to 40.
~B. 10x + 4y <= 40#This reverses the point values and uses the wrong inequality.
~C. x + y >= 40#This counts tasks instead of points.
=D. 4x + 10y >= 40#Correct. The total points must be at least 40.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the boundary line for 2x + y <= 8? {
~A. y = 2x + 8#This does not solve the related equation correctly.
=B. y = -2x + 8#Correct. Solve 2x + y = 8 for y.
~C. y = 8x + 2#This reverses the coefficients.
~D. y = 2x - 8#The slope and intercept signs are wrong.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Does (3, 1) satisfy 2x + y <= 8? {
~A. Yes, because 3 + 1 <= 8#This does not use the coefficient 2 on x.
~B. No, because 2(3) + 1 = 9#The arithmetic is incorrect; 6 + 1 = 7.
=C. Yes, because 2(3) + 1 <= 8#Correct. Substitution gives 7 <= 8.
~D. No, because 8 is greater than 7#If 7 is less than or equal to 8, the point is a solution.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality has a solid boundary and is shaded below the line? {
=A. y <= -3x + 1#Correct. The symbol <= uses a solid line and shades below.
~B. y < -3x + 1#This shades below but uses a dashed boundary.
~C. y >= -3x + 1#This uses a solid boundary but shades above.
~D. y > -3x + 1#This uses a dashed boundary and shades above.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>If a point lies in the shaded region of y > 2x - 1, what must be true? {
~A. The point must be on the boundary line#The boundary is dashed, so boundary points are not solutions.
~B. The x-value must be greater than the y-value#The comparison is between y and 2x - 1.
=C. The ordered pair makes y > 2x - 1 true#Correct. The shaded region is the solution set.
~D. The ordered pair makes y = 2x - 1 true#That describes the boundary, not the shaded solution region.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which test point is often easiest if it is not on the boundary line? {
~A. (1, 1)#This can work, but it is not usually the simplest default.
~B. (-1, 1)#This can work, but it is not usually the simplest default.
~C. (5, 5)#This usually creates more arithmetic than the origin.
=D. (0, 0)#Correct. The origin is often easiest because substitution is simple.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>A graph of x + y <= 12 represents a limit on two quantities. What does a point outside the shaded region mean? {
~A. The point uses exactly the allowed total#That would be on the boundary.
=B. The point does not satisfy the constraint#Correct. Points outside the shaded region make the inequality false.
~C. The point uses less than the allowed total#That would be inside the solution region.
~D. The point always has negative coordinates#Outside the region is about not satisfying the inequality, not coordinate signs.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph description matches y <= 1/3x - 2? {
=A. Solid boundary y = 1/3x - 2, shade below#Correct. The symbol <= includes equality and means y-values below or on the line.
~B. Dashed boundary y = 1/3x - 2, shade below#The shading is right, but the boundary should be solid.
~C. Solid boundary y = 1/3x - 2, shade above#The boundary is right, but the shading direction is wrong.
~D. Dashed boundary y = 1/3x - 2, shade above#Both the line type and shading direction are wrong.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y < -x + 3? {
~A. (0, 3)#Substitution gives 3 < 3, which is false because equality is not included.
=B. (0, 2)#Correct. Substitution gives 2 < 3.
~C. (1, 2)#Substitution gives 2 < 2, which is false.
~D. (3, 0)#Substitution gives 0 < 0, which is false.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality has boundary line y = -4x + 6 and shades above with a dashed line? {
~A. y >= -4x + 6#This shades above but uses a solid boundary.
~B. y <= -4x + 6#This uses a solid boundary and shades below.
=C. y > -4x + 6#Correct. Greater than shades above and uses a dashed boundary.
~D. y < -4x + 6#This uses a dashed boundary but shades below.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>A school can order at most 100 notebooks and folders combined. Which inequality models notebooks n and folders f? {
~A. n + f >= 100#At most means the total cannot be greater than 100.
~B. 100n + f <= 100#This treats 100 as a coefficient instead of the limit.
=C. n + f <= 100#Correct. The combined total must be less than or equal to 100.
~D. nf <= 100#The total combined count is a sum, not a product.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the boundary line for y >= -5? {
=A. y = -5#Correct. The boundary is the horizontal line where y equals -5.
~B. x = -5#This is a vertical line, not the boundary for y >= -5.
~C. y = 5x#This introduces an x-term that is not in the inequality.
~D. x + y = -5#This changes the structure of the inequality.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Does (2, 6) satisfy y >= 3x? {
~A. No, because 6 is less than 3(2)#6 is equal to 3(2), not less.
~B. No, because equality is not included#Equality is included with >=.
=C. Yes, because 6 >= 6 is true#Correct. Equality is included in >=.
~D. Yes, because 2 >= 6 is true#This compares the wrong values.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality matches a dashed line y = x - 4 shaded below? {
~A. y <= x - 4#This shades below but uses a solid boundary.
=B. y < x - 4#Correct. Less than uses a dashed boundary and shades below.
~C. y >= x - 4#This uses a solid boundary and shades above.
~D. y > x - 4#This uses a dashed boundary but shades above.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.4<br><br>Question:<br>For 5x + 2y <= 20, what does (2, 5) mean if x and y are item counts? {
~A. It is not a solution because 2 + 5 is less than 20#The weighted expression must be checked, not just x + y.
~B. It is a solution because 5(2) + 2(5) = 20#This is true, but it needs the interpretation that the limit is exactly met.
~C. It is outside the region because equality is not allowed#Equality is allowed with <=.
=D. It is a solution on the boundary because the total equals 20#Correct. The point satisfies the inequality exactly.




}

::ALG1_U03_L07_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which symbol requires a solid boundary line and shading above? {
=A. >=#Correct. Greater than or equal to includes the boundary and shades above when y is isolated.
~B. >#This shades above but uses a dashed boundary.
~C. <=#This uses a solid boundary but shades below when y is isolated.
~D. <#This uses a dashed boundary and shades below when y is isolated.




}
